1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,016
War is over. That is what the Seattle Star

2
00:00:04,056 --> 00:00:06,780
told its readers on Thursday, November

3
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:08,744
seventh, nineteen eighteen.

4
00:00:08,784 --> 00:00:10,709
There was just one problem.

5
00:00:10,749 --> 00:00:12,236
The war was not over.

6
00:00:12,276 --> 00:00:15,350
It would go on for four more days.

7
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:19,370
Paris, November seventh, by United Press

8
00:00:19,410 --> 00:00:22,531
leased wire. The allies and Germany signed

9
00:00:22,571 --> 00:00:25,165
an armistice at eleven o'clock this

10
00:00:25,205 --> 00:00:28,251
morning. Hostilities cease at two o'clock

11
00:00:28,291 --> 00:00:31,110
this afternoon. A place, a time, and a

12
00:00:31,150 --> 00:00:32,766
news agency behind it.

13
00:00:32,806 --> 00:00:35,701
It read like the most solid news of the

14
00:00:35,741 --> 00:00:36,642
war.

15
00:00:37,692 --> 00:00:40,524
In Kansas, the Topeka State Journal rushed

16
00:00:40,564 --> 00:00:42,028
out a third war extra.

17
00:00:42,068 --> 00:00:43,737
Reports of Hun surrender.

18
00:00:43,777 --> 00:00:46,609
Armistice with Germany is signed at eleven

19
00:00:46,649 --> 00:00:49,344
o'clock this morning, ending the world's

20
00:00:49,384 --> 00:00:52,148
greatest war. But look at the end of that

21
00:00:52,188 --> 00:00:54,292
line. A question mark.

22
00:00:55,342 --> 00:00:58,567
In Hammond, Indiana, the Lake County Times

23
00:00:58,607 --> 00:01:00,199
did not hedge at all.

24
00:01:00,239 --> 00:01:02,841
Hostilities ceased at two o'clock.

25
00:01:02,881 --> 00:01:04,181
War ends.

26
00:01:04,831 --> 00:01:07,353
From Seattle to Indiana, front pages

27
00:01:07,393 --> 00:01:10,128
printed the end of the First World War.

28
00:01:10,168 --> 00:01:12,334
People poured into the streets.

29
00:01:12,374 --> 00:01:15,181
And by that evening, the whole story had

30
00:01:15,221 --> 00:01:18,028
fallen apart. So where did the news come

31
00:01:18,068 --> 00:01:20,732
from? Who sent it? And why did so many

32
00:01:20,772 --> 00:01:23,650
papers believe it? The answer was waiting

33
00:01:23,690 --> 00:01:27,065
in a French port on the Atlantic coast.

34
00:01:27,815 --> 00:01:30,915
Part one. The flash.

35
00:01:31,965 --> 00:01:34,855
The Topeka paper printed who stood behind

36
00:01:34,895 --> 00:01:37,428
the story. It is true, United Press.

37
00:01:37,468 --> 00:01:40,358
The bulletin which brought the first news

38
00:01:40,398 --> 00:01:43,359
to America of the signing of the armistice

39
00:01:43,399 --> 00:01:45,574
was signed personally by Roy W.

40
00:01:45,614 --> 00:01:48,930
Howard, president of the United Press.

41
00:01:50,280 --> 00:01:51,502
This is Roy Howard.

42
00:01:51,542 --> 00:01:53,825
The photograph was made in nineteen

43
00:01:53,865 --> 00:01:56,548
fourteen. Four years later he was running

44
00:01:56,588 --> 00:01:59,270
the United Press, one of the biggest news

45
00:01:59,310 --> 00:02:01,859
wires in America, and he was in France.

46
00:02:01,899 --> 00:02:04,648
A bulletin with his own name on it was not

47
00:02:04,688 --> 00:02:07,147
a rumor. It was a guarantee.

48
00:02:08,197 --> 00:02:11,071
Newspapers raced to get the story out.

49
00:02:11,111 --> 00:02:14,293
In South Bend, Indiana, the News-Times set

50
00:02:14,333 --> 00:02:17,284
its headline so fast that it misspelled

51
00:02:17,324 --> 00:02:20,121
the word itself. Look at the letters.

52
00:02:20,161 --> 00:02:21,962
Two of them are swapped.

53
00:02:22,002 --> 00:02:23,496
Armistice is signed.

54
00:02:23,536 --> 00:02:26,130
Hostilities cease at once.

55
00:02:27,180 --> 00:02:29,858
Underneath, the paper added a second

56
00:02:29,898 --> 00:02:31,519
source. From New York

57
00:02:31,559 --> 00:02:34,313
the report that an armistice has been

58
00:02:34,353 --> 00:02:37,181
signed is a Wall Street report, but is

59
00:02:37,221 --> 00:02:40,202
guaranteed to be absolutely reliable, by

60
00:02:40,242 --> 00:02:43,297
the Dow, Jones and Company financial news

61
00:02:43,337 --> 00:02:46,468
agency. Wall Street and the Stock Exchange

62
00:02:46,508 --> 00:02:48,733
are in mid air in consequence

63
00:02:48,773 --> 00:02:51,602
particularly since Washington declines

64
00:02:51,642 --> 00:02:53,903
official confirmation.

65
00:02:54,553 --> 00:02:57,683
Washington declines official confirmation.

66
00:02:57,723 --> 00:03:00,701
Buried at the end of a paragraph, it was

67
00:03:00,741 --> 00:03:02,890
the first crack in the story.

68
00:03:02,930 --> 00:03:06,171
And it was about to get much wider.

69
00:03:06,921 --> 00:03:10,407
Part two. New York goes wild.

70
00:03:11,057 --> 00:03:13,496
Topeka's front page tracked the

71
00:03:13,536 --> 00:03:15,495
celebration city by city.

72
00:03:15,535 --> 00:03:17,014
Washington is wild.

73
00:03:17,054 --> 00:03:19,972
Celebrate in Chi. New York goes wild.

74
00:03:20,012 --> 00:03:23,250
Broadway crazy after news of surrender is

75
00:03:23,290 --> 00:03:24,450
spread.

76
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:28,011
A New York report printed in Omaha the

77
00:03:28,051 --> 00:03:29,487
next day described it.

78
00:03:29,527 --> 00:03:32,105
Sophisticated New York became delirious

79
00:03:32,145 --> 00:03:34,790
with joy today over the unfounded report

80
00:03:34,830 --> 00:03:37,206
that Germany had agreed to armistice

81
00:03:37,246 --> 00:03:39,287
terms. The celebration seemed a

82
00:03:39,327 --> 00:03:41,904
combination of Fourth of July, election

83
00:03:41,944 --> 00:03:44,522
night, New Year's eve, Thanksgiving and

84
00:03:44,562 --> 00:03:45,833
Christmas.

85
00:03:46,883 --> 00:03:49,450
Back in Hammond, the Times made its own

86
00:03:49,490 --> 00:03:51,055
speed part of the story.

87
00:03:51,095 --> 00:03:52,926
Country mad with jubilation.

88
00:03:52,966 --> 00:03:55,533
United Press scoops all news collecting

89
00:03:55,573 --> 00:03:58,580
agencies all over the United States.

90
00:03:59,230 --> 00:04:01,713
Across the top of the page, the Times

91
00:04:01,753 --> 00:04:03,145
congratulated itself.

92
00:04:03,185 --> 00:04:05,464
Times extra gives first real news.

93
00:04:05,504 --> 00:04:08,328
For a newspaper, a scoop is the best thing

94
00:04:08,368 --> 00:04:11,192
that can happen. Unless it turns out to be

95
00:04:11,232 --> 00:04:13,852
wrong. And on that same page, there was

96
00:04:13,892 --> 00:04:15,719
already a warning.

97
00:04:16,469 --> 00:04:19,472
Part three. The denial.

98
00:04:20,522 --> 00:04:23,240
In the middle of the Hammond front page

99
00:04:23,280 --> 00:04:25,860
below the flag and the words war ends

100
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:28,549
State Department had no confirmation at

101
00:04:28,589 --> 00:04:29,741
two p.m.

102
00:04:30,791 --> 00:04:33,719
Topeka printed the same doubt, right below

103
00:04:33,759 --> 00:04:36,263
Howard's bulletin. Doubt on reports.

104
00:04:36,303 --> 00:04:39,090
It was officially announced at the state

105
00:04:39,130 --> 00:04:41,775
department at two fifteen o'clock this

106
00:04:41,815 --> 00:04:44,672
afternoon that the Germans had not signed

107
00:04:44,712 --> 00:04:47,569
armistice terms. The word not, in capital

108
00:04:47,609 --> 00:04:50,113
letters, on the same page as Germans

109
00:04:50,153 --> 00:04:51,248
beaten.

110
00:04:52,298 --> 00:04:55,466
In Oklahoma City, the Times, on Associated

111
00:04:55,506 --> 00:04:58,444
Press wire, ran the story the other way

112
00:04:58,484 --> 00:05:01,194
round. Early unofficial reports that

113
00:05:01,234 --> 00:05:03,943
armistice had been signed officially

114
00:05:03,983 --> 00:05:06,998
denied by Secretary Lansing, after cable

115
00:05:07,038 --> 00:05:09,136
inquiries to French capital.

116
00:05:09,176 --> 00:05:11,533
Rejoicing is premature.

117
00:05:12,183 --> 00:05:14,559
In El Paso, Texas, the Herald said it

118
00:05:14,599 --> 00:05:16,910
outright. Report armistice is signed

119
00:05:16,950 --> 00:05:19,183
officially denied by U.S.

120
00:05:20,233 --> 00:05:22,449
Secretary of State Robert Lansing

121
00:05:22,489 --> 00:05:23,954
explained how he knew.

122
00:05:23,994 --> 00:05:26,552
When the report reached his department

123
00:05:26,592 --> 00:05:29,013
that morning, an inquiry was sent to

124
00:05:29,053 --> 00:05:31,680
Paris. At two oh four in the afternoon

125
00:05:31,720 --> 00:05:33,047
the reply came back.

126
00:05:33,087 --> 00:05:35,372
The armistice had not been signed.

127
00:05:35,412 --> 00:05:38,107
And the German representatives would not

128
00:05:38,147 --> 00:05:40,774
even meet Marshal Foch until five p.m.

129
00:05:40,814 --> 00:05:42,166
Paris time.

130
00:05:42,816 --> 00:05:45,741
The German envoys had not even sat down at

131
00:05:45,781 --> 00:05:48,564
the table. So where had the United Press

132
00:05:48,604 --> 00:05:50,329
found a signed armistice?

133
00:05:50,369 --> 00:05:53,152
The answer came the next morning, and it

134
00:05:53,192 --> 00:05:55,133
came from the Navy.

135
00:05:55,883 --> 00:05:57,700
And now, part four.

136
00:05:57,740 --> 00:05:59,513
The admiral.

137
00:06:00,563 --> 00:06:02,966
On Friday, New York's Evening World

138
00:06:03,006 --> 00:06:05,340
printed it. Admiral Wilson assumes

139
00:06:05,380 --> 00:06:08,132
responsibility for report that armistice

140
00:06:08,172 --> 00:06:10,855
was signed. Commander at Brest says the

141
00:06:10,895 --> 00:06:13,648
information appeared to be official, and

142
00:06:13,688 --> 00:06:15,963
exonerates United Press.

143
00:06:17,313 --> 00:06:19,895
This is Henry B. Wilson, photographed a

144
00:06:19,935 --> 00:06:22,718
few years later, in nineteen twenty three.

145
00:06:22,758 --> 00:06:25,474
In November nineteen eighteen, he was the

146
00:06:25,514 --> 00:06:28,230
admiral commanding the American forces in

147
00:06:28,270 --> 00:06:30,650
French waters, at the port of Brest.

148
00:06:30,690 --> 00:06:34,046
That is where Roy Howard was on Thursday.

149
00:06:35,096 --> 00:06:36,542
The admiral's statement

150
00:06:36,582 --> 00:06:39,141
The statement of the United Press relative

151
00:06:39,181 --> 00:06:41,618
to the signing of the armistice was made

152
00:06:41,658 --> 00:06:43,970
public from my office, on the basis of

153
00:06:44,010 --> 00:06:45,951
what appeared to be official and

154
00:06:45,991 --> 00:06:48,200
authoritative information.

155
00:06:49,250 --> 00:06:51,831
I am in a position to know that the United

156
00:06:51,871 --> 00:06:54,141
Press and its representative acted in

157
00:06:54,181 --> 00:06:56,762
perfect good faith, and that the premature

158
00:06:56,802 --> 00:06:59,196
announcement was the result of an error

159
00:06:59,236 --> 00:07:01,381
for which the agency was in no wise

160
00:07:01,421 --> 00:07:02,770
responsible.

161
00:07:03,420 --> 00:07:05,640
In Paris, the President's special

162
00:07:05,680 --> 00:07:08,449
representative, Edward House, looked into

163
00:07:08,489 --> 00:07:10,777
it. In a telegram to Washington on

164
00:07:10,817 --> 00:07:13,654
November eighth, he traced the report to a

165
00:07:13,694 --> 00:07:16,325
wire from the American naval attaché in

166
00:07:16,365 --> 00:07:18,586
Paris to Admiral Wilson at Brest.

167
00:07:18,626 --> 00:07:21,326
Wilson showed it to Howard, and Howard's

168
00:07:21,366 --> 00:07:23,723
dispatch got past the cable censor.

169
00:07:23,763 --> 00:07:26,394
House wrote: It is perfectly clear that

170
00:07:26,434 --> 00:07:28,929
United Press was not at fault in this

171
00:07:28,969 --> 00:07:31,668
matter. The blame, he said, lay with the

172
00:07:31,708 --> 00:07:34,408
attaché, or with the French official who

173
00:07:34,448 --> 00:07:36,144
began the rumor.

174
00:07:37,194 --> 00:07:40,003
Its great rival, the Associated Press, was

175
00:07:40,043 --> 00:07:42,784
less forgiving. In Omaha, the Bee printed

176
00:07:42,824 --> 00:07:45,565
the verdict. Armistice report is greatest

177
00:07:45,605 --> 00:07:47,629
hoax of recent years.

178
00:07:48,679 --> 00:07:51,408
And next to it: Whole country duped by

179
00:07:51,448 --> 00:07:53,505
false news of peace.

180
00:07:54,779 --> 00:07:57,796
The same front page held a clue to how a

181
00:07:57,836 --> 00:07:59,936
rumor like this could start.

182
00:07:59,976 --> 00:08:03,070
Fighting stopped on one sector of western

183
00:08:03,110 --> 00:08:05,898
front, to permit passage of Germany's

184
00:08:05,938 --> 00:08:08,802
armistice envoys. A pause in the guns.

185
00:08:08,842 --> 00:08:11,200
Not the end of the war.

186
00:08:12,250 --> 00:08:14,694
Because the war was still going on.

187
00:08:14,734 --> 00:08:17,605
Friday's Evening World said so across the

188
00:08:17,645 --> 00:08:20,231
whole page. Foch won't stop fighting.

189
00:08:20,271 --> 00:08:22,717
Must take terms by Monday.

190
00:08:23,467 --> 00:08:27,028
Part five. Monday.

191
00:08:27,678 --> 00:08:30,472
Early on Monday, November eleventh, the

192
00:08:30,512 --> 00:08:31,852
real telegram came.

193
00:08:31,892 --> 00:08:34,613
Edward House cabled the President from

194
00:08:34,653 --> 00:08:37,592
Paris. Armistice signed five o'clock this

195
00:08:37,632 --> 00:08:40,571
morning. Hostilities to cease eleven a.m.

196
00:08:40,611 --> 00:08:41,647
today.

197
00:08:42,697 --> 00:08:45,849
That afternoon, the Evening World printed

198
00:08:45,889 --> 00:08:48,028
it. Foch calls off fighting.

199
00:08:48,068 --> 00:08:50,986
Thousands of U.S. guns fire their last

200
00:08:51,026 --> 00:08:53,183
shots at eleven a.m.

201
00:08:54,533 --> 00:08:56,638
And New York celebrated again.

202
00:08:56,678 --> 00:08:59,427
Bain News Service photographers were in

203
00:08:59,467 --> 00:09:01,143
the streets that Monday.

204
00:09:01,183 --> 00:09:03,788
Look at the edge of this glass plate.

205
00:09:03,828 --> 00:09:06,219
Someone wrote the caption by hand.

206
00:09:06,259 --> 00:09:09,648
Peace hurrah. Eleven, eleven, eighteen.

207
00:09:10,998 --> 00:09:13,956
A parade, with flags and a man in a top

208
00:09:13,996 --> 00:09:16,570
hat, past a crowd on the sidewalk.

209
00:09:16,610 --> 00:09:20,131
People in the windows above, watching.

210
00:09:21,481 --> 00:09:24,466
A street of tall buildings, hung with

211
00:09:24,506 --> 00:09:27,410
flags, and packed from wall to wall.

212
00:09:27,450 --> 00:09:30,421
This time, the news was true.

213
00:09:31,471 --> 00:09:34,451
In Seattle, the Star put out an eighth

214
00:09:34,491 --> 00:09:36,597
extra. Armistice is signed.

215
00:09:36,637 --> 00:09:39,538
War ends. Four days earlier, the same

216
00:09:39,578 --> 00:09:42,399
newspaper had told the same readers

217
00:09:42,439 --> 00:09:43,993
war is over.

218
00:09:44,743 --> 00:09:47,663
Printed: an armistice signed on Thursday

219
00:09:47,703 --> 00:09:49,756
November seventh. The record

220
00:09:49,796 --> 00:09:52,788
an armistice signed at five in the morning

221
00:09:52,828 --> 00:09:54,881
on Monday, November eleventh.

222
00:09:54,921 --> 00:09:56,676
Four days apart.

223
00:09:57,326 --> 00:10:00,178
So the papers that Thursday did not invent

224
00:10:00,218 --> 00:10:03,071
a peace. They had a bulletin signed by the

225
00:10:03,111 --> 00:10:05,619
head of a news wire, released from an

226
00:10:05,659 --> 00:10:08,512
American admiral's office, and passed by a

227
00:10:08,552 --> 00:10:11,129
censor. Every link in the chain looked

228
00:10:11,169 --> 00:10:13,883
solid. Nobody had checked the first one.

229
00:10:13,923 --> 00:10:16,294
If you had read war is over on that

230
00:10:16,334 --> 00:10:19,186
Thursday, would you have gone out into the

231
00:10:19,226 --> 00:10:22,030
street? Tell me in the comments.

232
00:10:23,180 --> 00:10:25,833
If you like stories told from the papers

233
00:10:25,873 --> 00:10:28,458
themselves, give this video a like, and

234
00:10:28,498 --> 00:10:30,949
subscribe to As Printed, for what the

235
00:10:30,989 --> 00:10:34,214
papers said, and what the archive says.
